speechtext:
Deputy Minister of Finance.
There had been no word from Mr. McCarthy to show any desire on hi3 part to withdraw his objections to the certificate, yet we find the deputy minister writing to him and all but suggesting that he should withdraw all objections. All the facts land circumstances show that the Finance Department displayed a great deal of anxiety, not to safeguard the interests of the shareholders and depositors or the general public, but to get the certificate into the hands of Tiravers so that he might commence operations under the name of the Farmers' Bank.
Then we find on November 2, 1906, a letter from Mr. McCarthy stating that his clients had been settled with and withdrawing his objection; and because of that letter the Minister of Finance s_ays_ h(* was entitled to proceed as if no objections had been made. But the facts set out in the writ of summons, a copy of which was in the hands of the Minister of Finance and on file in his department, showed^ most serious accusations. It is not my intention to read them in full, but I desire to set out briefly what they meant.
The charges made in this writ were:
(a.) That the subscriptions were procured through deceit, fraud and misrepresentation;
(b.) The particulars of the fraud are stated. We cite one example, namely, that John Sproat, one of the plaintiffs, intended to subscribe for $1,000, and his application was wrongfully entered at $10,000;
(e.) That the directors mentioned in the prospectus have neither subscribed for shares, nor consented to act as directors;
(d.) That agents who procured subscriptions for shares had taken promissory notes on account of subscriptions, which were payable to the provisional directors, and that they were endeavouring to discount these notes in order to make up the deposit of $250,000.
(e.) That a large sum of money paid oh account of capital had been unlawfully deducted for commissions;
(f.) That the money and promissory notes taken from subscribers were impressed with a trust under the Bank Act for all the subscribers;
(g.) An affidavit of verification was filed, and notice of motion served and an injunction claimed to restrain the further publication of a notice calling a meeting, and to restrain the directors from making any application to the Treasury Board for a certificate to open the bank.
Now, can any reasonable man who peruses the records on file say that these charges should have been disregarded when the application for certificate was being considered? I ask, was the department concerned only about Mr. McCarthy's clients? What about the hundreds of other poor shareholders and depositors? When the government delayed the certificate in order to get Mr. McCarthy's clients out of the hole, did they discharge their full duty towards the public and towards the shareholders and depositors throughout the length and breadth of the land? Had they still no duty , to protect others from fraud
